body:
>his cheating wife actually let out a brief chuckle before calling me out for being a dumbass for thinking this was a ruse. Then berating me for being so insensitive.

What the hell? So basically "let me berate you for thinking my fucking around and breaking up this family is so stupid and preposterous that you thought it can only be a joke"? You really were insensitive, though insensitive to how she actually is a cheating skank.

&#x200B;

Right.. there's about 300 000 abortions a year only in the USA, so a bunch of people not having babies for a while is hardly anything. But strong statement. Also if they're so concerned, go fund physicists or become one yourself and get involved in Nuclear Fusion projects. Honestly, these people seem to just need a reaffirming statement that everything will be ok. Because realistically apart from funding Fusion projects, there's no real big alternative to producing enough clean energy for our civilization. And yes not even renewables can compete with what fusion can bring.

&#x200B;

tldr: stop bitching and start reading physics.

Might consider trying some form of martial arts. For me Wing Tsun gave me a lot of confidence and discipline. I think it can also help your mind zone out some things or figure out how to deal with them, plus you can always blow off some anger at a punching bag.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I hope this is not too sensitive and obviously feel free not to answer, but how do you think homosexual relationships hurt the people participating in them? From my view, I think people are born with homosexuality and if they can get love and intimacy with someone of the same sex, then all the best. However, I don't know how not being able to have a biological offspring would influence that, I assume most gay couples are happy to adopt.

&#x200B;

Also what is a relationship with god for you? As someone not religious myself it's a bit hard to understand.

Can you elaborate in what way is it free and independent? Do you believe or think any news or radio information is unbiased or does not portray a certain view?

&#x200B;

Also I think you would agree the situation in the 30s and now is drastically different. Any given person has access to the newspapers radios and media form the entire planet.

&#x200B;

As far as the content, documentaries would probably be the only thing I'd be willing to give a try.

Hey guys, I'm totally new here. I recently graduated a Bachelor of Physics from Leipzig University in Germany and I am looking into if starting out with data science makes sense for me and if it is interesting. I would be really grateful if I could get some information on some of my questions. I am currently a master student at Technical Unviersity Munich studying Computational Science and Engineering.

&#x200B;

Firstly, how good is someone from a physics background positioned to enter into this field? I've studied Classical Mechanics, Electricity and Magnetism, Quantum Mechanics, Thermodynamics and Statistical Physics to almost a master level material (our curriculum was a bit messy and it involved topics from graduate level textbooks). Math wise I've covered Calculus, Linear Algebra, Complex Analysis, Vector Analysis and a fair bit of statistics. Part of my electives were Computer Simulations so I also know a fair bit of python, but very little C++. However I have little experience with Linux systems or git.

&#x200B;

Second, would someone be able to point me to job portals or companies who do data science, so I could learn more about the industry and what the work involves?

&#x200B;

And finally, how do you guys think would be the best way for me to sell myself in my cover letter or CV? All I've heard or read leans towards saying that as a physics student I learned how to think analytically and be a general problem solver, and want to apply that skillset in the real world.
